      "text": "Given that two of the roots of the polynomial \\(ax^3 + bx^2 + cx + d = 0\\) are \\(3\\) and \\(-2\\), we can express the polynomial in its factored form as:\n\\[a(x - 3)(x + 2)(x - r) = 0.\\]\nWe need to expand this expression to find the coefficients. First, we expand \\((x - 3)(x + 2)\\):\n\\[(x - 3)(x + 2) = x^2 + 2x - 3x - 6 = x^2 - x - 6.\\]\nNow, we multiply this result by \\((x - r)\\):\n\\[(x^2 - x - 6)(x - r) = x^3 - rx^2 - x^2 + rx - 6x + 6r = x^3 - (r+1)x^2 + (r-6)x + 6r.\\]\nThus, the polynomial \\(a(x - 3)(x + 2)(x - r)\\) can be written as:\n\\[a(x^3 - (r+1)x^2 + (r-6)x + 6r) = ax^3 - a(r+1)x^2 + a(r-6)x + 6ar.\\]\nBy comparing this with the general form \\(ax^3 + bx^2 + cx + d\\), we identify the coefficients:\n\\[b = -a(r+1), \\quad c = a(r-6), \\quad d = 6ar.\\]\nWe need to find the value of \\(\\frac{b+c}{a}\\):\n\\[\\frac{b+c}{a} = \\frac{-a(r+1) + a(r-6)}{a} = \\frac{-a r - a + a r - 6a}{a} = \\frac{-a - 6a}{a} = \\frac{-7a}{a} = -7.\\]\nTherefore, the answer is:\n\\[\\boxed{-7}.\\]",
