      "text": "Given that two of the roots of the cubic equation \\(ax^3 + bx^2 + cx + d = 0\\) are \\(3\\) and \\(-2\\), we can express the polynomial as:\n\\[\na(x - 3)(x + 2)(x - r) = 0\n\\]\nwhere \\(r\\) is the third root of the polynomial. Expanding the factored form, we get:\n\\[\na(x - 3)(x + 2)(x - r) = a \\left( (x - 3)(x + 2) \\right)(x - r) = a \\left( x^2 - x - 6 \\right)(x - r)\n\\]\nNext, we expand \\( (x^2 - x - 6)(x - r) \\):\n\\[\n(x^2 - x - 6)(x - r) = x^3 - rx^2 - x^2 + rx - 6x + 6r = x^3 - (r+1)x^2 + (r-6)x + 6r\n\\]\nTherefore, the polynomial can be written as:\n\\[\na(x^3 - (r+1)x^2 + (r-6)x + 6r) = ax^3 - a(r+1)x^2 + a(r-6)x + 6ar\n\\]\nBy comparing coefficients with the original polynomial \\(ax^3 + bx^2 + cx + d\\), we identify:\n\\[\nb = -a(r+1), \\quad c = a(r-6), \\quad d = 6ar\n\\]\nWe are asked to find \\(\\frac{b+c}{a}\\). Substituting the expressions for \\(b\\) and \\(c\\), we get:\n\\[\n\\frac{b+c}{a} = \\frac{-a(r+1) + a(r-6)}{a} = \\frac{-ar - a + ar - 6a}{a} = \\frac{-a - 6a}{a} = \\frac{-7a}{a} = -7\n\\]\nThus, the final answer is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{-7}\n\\]",
